  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 204 PM MDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nWednesday morning, ensembles are favoring a strong 500-mb high \nacross the Southeastern United States, with troughing to the \nwest. At the same time, a strong surface high supported by an \nactive jet stream looks to be located in the Great Lakes Region.\nThese features are favored to remain in place through Friday. \nSoutherly flow would be expected across the Great Plains between\nthe southeastern high and western trough, however the surface \nhigh in the Great Lakes Region is forecast to support easterly \nsurface flow across Kansas. Warm, wet conditions look to be in \nplace across the area Wednesday through Friday as a result of \nthis pattern. High temperatures all three days are in the upper \n70s to mid 80s. It's important to note that NBM 75th percentile \nand higher scenarios show highs in the upper 80s to lower 90s \nThursday and Friday. This could occur if the southerly flow can \nreach further north into Southern Nebraska, or if solar heating \ncan develop. Even so, rain is in the forecast, as NBM 24 hr \nprecipitation guidance shows a 40% chance or greater of \nreceiving more than 0.1 inches of rain both Wednesday and \nThursday across most of the forecast area. These probabilities \nare highest along a line from Norton, KS to Tribune, KS \nWednesday (50-70%), and in Northeastern Colorado and far \nSouthwestern Nebraska Thursday (60- 75%). Most of this activity \nis forecast to be widespread rain rather than localized \nconvective storms, the exception being Wednesday. LREF guidance \nsuggests a 40-55% chance across most of the forecast area of \nexperiencing surface CAPE (a measure of atmospheric instability)\ngreater than 1000 J/kg Wednesday evening. Some higher end \nscenarios (90th percentile and greater) show CAPE may rise above\n2000 J/kg. Even so, this convective activity is not favored to \nbe severe, as GFS and EC soundings show sfc-6km shear remaining \nbelow 30 kts during the afternoon and evening hours. Confidence \nin severe weather occurring Wednesday is 5% or less. \n\nThe forecast begins to become a bit more uncertain by Saturday \nmorning, specifically regarding the trough to the west of the \nforecast area. This feature is expected to be moving slowly \neastward throughout the long term period, though guidance is \ninconsistent on when it will move over the forecast area. \nHowever, once it moves over the area, temperatures may drop a \nbit more, in addition to precipitation chances. This is \nrepresented in the forecast temperatures for both Saturday and \nSunday, with highs in the mid to upper 70s. Additionally, NBM 24\nhr precipitation guidance drops the probabilities for greater \nthan 0.1 inches of rain below 40% on Sunday. These probabilities\nare a little higher on Saturday, but is likely due to a higher \nchance that the trough is still west of the forecast area on \nSaturday. In other words, conditions similar to Wednesday \nthrough Friday are expected as long as the trough stays to the \nwest of the forecast area. However, once it moves in over the \nregion, temperatures and precipitation would likely drop off a \nlittle bit.",
